1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,360
Here's an experiment you
can do with your lunch.

2
00:00:02,615 --> 00:00:03,765
Take a plain cracker,

3
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:05,210
with no added sugar.

4
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:06,680
Chew it, and keep chewing.

5
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:07,660
Don't swallow.

6
00:00:07,845 --> 00:00:09,320
Give it a minute or so,

7
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,590
and it starts to
taste a little sweet.

8
00:00:11,610 --> 00:00:13,080
But you didn't add any sugar.

9
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:15,470
So where's that
sweetness coming from?

10
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:17,545
A molecule in your saliva.

11
00:00:17,565 --> 00:00:21,370
In a minute, it does what would
otherwise take at least a million years.

12
00:00:24,234 --> 00:00:26,324
Crackers are packed with starch.

13
00:00:26,344 --> 00:00:29,499
Zoom right in, and starch
turns out to be a chain.

14
00:00:29,519 --> 00:00:30,964
Hundreds, even thousands,

15
00:00:30,984 --> 00:00:32,524
of glucose molecules,

16
00:00:32,544 --> 00:00:34,164
linked end to end.

17
00:00:34,184 --> 00:00:35,434
Glucose is a sugar,

18
00:00:35,454 --> 00:00:37,229
but locked up in a chain like this,

19
00:00:37,259 --> 00:00:38,639
it doesn't taste sweet.

20
00:00:38,809 --> 00:00:40,589
And those links are tough.

21
00:00:40,609 --> 00:00:42,879
Leave one in water
at room temperature,

22
00:00:42,979 --> 00:00:45,364
and on average it would
last millions of years.

23
00:00:45,517 --> 00:00:46,782
Enter amylase:

24
00:00:46,877 --> 00:00:49,552
an enzyme made in
your salivary glands.

25
00:00:49,572 --> 00:00:51,087
It's a carbohydrase:

26
00:00:51,232 --> 00:00:53,452
it snips starch into smaller sugars,

27
00:00:53,472 --> 00:00:57,317
like maltose, which is just two
glucose units joined together.

28
00:00:57,337 --> 00:00:59,217
And maltose does taste sweet.

29
00:00:59,497 --> 00:01:01,057
That's what an enzyme is:

30
00:01:01,097 --> 00:01:02,957
a biological catalyst.

31
00:01:03,187 --> 00:01:06,917
It speeds up a reaction without
being used up or changed.

32
00:01:06,937 --> 00:01:09,422
Nearly every reaction
in your body has one,

33
00:01:09,482 --> 00:01:12,117
whether it's building things
up or breaking them down.

34
00:01:12,192 --> 00:01:16,832
Without enzymes, your metabolism would
be far too slow to keep you alive.

35
00:01:16,852 --> 00:01:19,147
Nearly all enzymes are proteins:

36
00:01:19,282 --> 00:01:21,417
long chains of amino acids,

37
00:01:21,527 --> 00:01:23,512
folded into a precise shape.

38
00:01:23,532 --> 00:01:25,212
And that shape is everything.

39
00:01:25,398 --> 00:01:28,498
Every enzyme has a small
pocket called the active site.

40
00:01:28,748 --> 00:01:30,148
The molecule it works on,

41
00:01:30,168 --> 00:01:32,278
the substrate, fits inside,

42
00:01:32,338 --> 00:01:35,068
forming an enzyme–substrate complex.

43
00:01:35,088 --> 00:01:38,288
Held there, a bond becomes
much easier to break.

44
00:01:38,308 --> 00:01:40,278
Snap. The products leave,

45
00:01:40,298 --> 00:01:43,003
and the active site is
free for the next one.

46
00:01:43,023 --> 00:01:45,773
The active site is
complementary to the substrate:

47
00:01:45,938 --> 00:01:47,068
not the same shape,

48
00:01:47,088 --> 00:01:48,288
but a perfect fit.

49
00:01:48,488 --> 00:01:50,108
The enzyme is the lock.

50
00:01:50,263 --> 00:01:51,788
The substrate is the key.

51
00:01:51,883 --> 00:01:54,253
That's why enzymes are specific.

52
00:01:54,273 --> 00:01:56,918
Each one only fits its own substrate.

53
00:01:56,938 --> 00:01:59,808
Amylase can't even digest paper.

54
00:01:59,828 --> 00:02:01,288
It's mostly cellulose,

55
00:02:01,308 --> 00:02:02,768
another chain of glucose,

56
00:02:02,833 --> 00:02:04,753
but its links are joined differently,

57
00:02:04,808 --> 00:02:06,183
so it just doesn't fit.

58
00:02:06,363 --> 00:02:08,093
Lock and key is a brilliant model,

59
00:02:08,113 --> 00:02:09,613
but it's a simplification.

60
00:02:09,848 --> 00:02:11,753
Real enzymes aren't rigid.

61
00:02:12,038 --> 00:02:13,318
As the substrate binds,

62
00:02:13,338 --> 00:02:15,513
the active site moulds
itself around it.

63
00:02:15,778 --> 00:02:18,448
That more accurate picture
is called induced fit.

64
00:02:18,743 --> 00:02:21,528
For GCSE and IGCSE,

65
00:02:21,578 --> 00:02:23,328
lock and key is all you need.

66
00:02:23,583 --> 00:02:26,083
Induced fit is an A-level idea.

67
00:02:26,103 --> 00:02:27,133
Just remember:

68
00:02:27,213 --> 00:02:29,563
it's a model, not a photograph.

69
00:02:29,976 --> 00:02:31,726
Which brings us to myth number one:

70
00:02:31,871 --> 00:02:33,431
that enzymes get used up.

71
00:02:33,576 --> 00:02:34,571
They don't.

72
00:02:34,591 --> 00:02:35,951
After each reaction,

73
00:02:35,971 --> 00:02:37,771
the enzyme is unchanged,

74
00:02:37,796 --> 00:02:38,646
ready to go again,

75
00:02:38,666 --> 00:02:39,481
and again.

76
00:02:39,691 --> 00:02:41,886
And some are astonishingly fast.

77
00:02:42,091 --> 00:02:44,776
Catalase is found in
almost all your cells.

78
00:02:45,056 --> 00:02:48,646
One molecule of it can break down
hundreds of thousands of molecules of

79
00:02:48,666 --> 00:02:50,191
hydrogen peroxide.

80
00:02:50,426 --> 00:02:51,406
Every second.

81
00:02:51,586 --> 00:02:54,006
Your cells do replace old enzymes,

82
00:02:54,026 --> 00:02:57,631
eventually. But the reaction
itself never uses them up.

83
00:02:58,022 --> 00:02:59,302
Now, temperature.

84
00:02:59,477 --> 00:03:01,817
Molecules are always jiggling around.

85
00:03:02,017 --> 00:03:04,617
Heat them up, and they
gain kinetic energy.

86
00:03:04,637 --> 00:03:05,797
They move faster.

87
00:03:06,057 --> 00:03:09,167
Enzymes and substrates bump
into each other more often,

88
00:03:09,187 --> 00:03:10,482
and with more energy.

89
00:03:10,672 --> 00:03:12,717
So more collisions are successful,

90
00:03:12,737 --> 00:03:15,482
with the substrate landing
in the active site.

91
00:03:15,502 --> 00:03:17,987
More enzyme–substrate complexes form,

92
00:03:18,007 --> 00:03:19,467
and the rate goes up.

93
00:03:19,487 --> 00:03:22,012
Up to a point, warmer means faster.

94
00:03:22,352 --> 00:03:25,222
The temperature where the rate
peaks is called the optimum.

95
00:03:25,532 --> 00:03:27,262
For many enzymes in your body,

96
00:03:27,282 --> 00:03:29,117
it's around 37 degrees:

97
00:03:29,257 --> 00:03:30,417
body temperature.

98
00:03:30,716 --> 00:03:32,451
So what if we keep
turning up the heat?

99
00:03:32,531 --> 00:03:33,556
Fifty degrees.

100
00:03:33,671 --> 00:03:35,316
Sixty. Seventy.

101
00:03:35,376 --> 00:03:37,451
Does it just keep getting faster?

102
00:03:37,471 --> 00:03:40,611
Pause the video, and
make your prediction.

103
00:03:43,576 --> 00:03:45,181
Here's what actually happens.

104
00:03:45,356 --> 00:03:47,551
The rate crashes, and
it doesn't recover.

105
00:03:47,741 --> 00:03:51,356
An enzyme's shape is held
together by lots of weak bonds.

106
00:03:51,376 --> 00:03:55,786
Too much heat, and it vibrates so
violently that those bonds break.

107
00:03:55,806 --> 00:03:57,166
The chain unravels,

108
00:03:57,186 --> 00:03:59,101
the active site loses its shape,

109
00:03:59,256 --> 00:04:01,306
and the substrate no longer fits.

110
00:04:01,541 --> 00:04:03,751
The enzyme has been denatured.

111
00:04:03,771 --> 00:04:08,181
It's the same kind of change that
turns egg white solid in a hot pan.

112
00:04:08,201 --> 00:04:09,606
You can't uncook an egg,

113
00:04:09,721 --> 00:04:12,411
and cooling usually won't
fix the enzyme either.

114
00:04:12,606 --> 00:04:13,891
Cold is different.

115
00:04:14,096 --> 00:04:16,246
Molecules have less kinetic energy,

116
00:04:16,266 --> 00:04:17,856
so there are fewer collisions.

117
00:04:18,101 --> 00:04:20,086
But the enzyme isn't denatured.

118
00:04:20,276 --> 00:04:22,021
Warm it up, and it recovers.

119
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:24,311
And that's myth number two:

120
00:04:24,331 --> 00:04:26,041
that heat "kills" enzymes.

121
00:04:26,271 --> 00:04:27,841
Enzymes were never alive.

122
00:04:28,111 --> 00:04:29,796
An enzyme is a molecule,

123
00:04:29,816 --> 00:04:30,831
a very clever one,

124
00:04:30,851 --> 00:04:31,976
but it can't die.

125
00:04:32,226 --> 00:04:33,611
It's denatured.

126
00:04:33,631 --> 00:04:35,496
Its active site changes shape,

127
00:04:35,516 --> 00:04:37,371
so the substrate no longer fits,

128
00:04:37,391 --> 00:04:40,456
and no enzyme–substrate
complex can form.

129
00:04:40,476 --> 00:04:43,216
In an exam, that's the
answer that gets the marks.

130
00:04:43,487 --> 00:04:46,762
pH, how acidic or
alkaline something is,

131
00:04:46,877 --> 00:04:49,222
can denature enzymes too.

132
00:04:49,242 --> 00:04:50,597
Too far either way,

133
00:04:50,672 --> 00:04:54,112
and the bonds holding the active
site in shape are disrupted.

134
00:04:54,132 --> 00:04:56,742
So every enzyme has an optimum pH.

135
00:04:57,007 --> 00:04:58,232
Amylase, in your mouth,

136
00:04:58,252 --> 00:04:59,912
works best close to neutral,

137
00:04:59,932 --> 00:05:01,747
around pH 7.

138
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:03,752
Your stomach is strongly acidic,

139
00:05:03,772 --> 00:05:05,922
often around pH 2.

140
00:05:05,942 --> 00:05:09,212
And pepsin, a protease that
digests protein there,

141
00:05:09,232 --> 00:05:11,162
works best right around that.

142
00:05:11,182 --> 00:05:12,232
Different enzymes,

143
00:05:12,252 --> 00:05:13,542
tuned to different places.

144
00:05:14,148 --> 00:05:15,658
So, back to that cracker.

145
00:05:15,878 --> 00:05:18,298
As you chew, amylase
meets the starch.

146
00:05:18,538 --> 00:05:20,453
The chains slot into its active site,

147
00:05:20,473 --> 00:05:21,993
and it snips them into maltose,

148
00:05:22,013 --> 00:05:24,138
over and over, without being used up.

149
00:05:24,158 --> 00:05:26,153
Your mouth is about 37 degrees,

150
00:05:26,173 --> 00:05:27,608
and close to neutral.

151
00:05:27,628 --> 00:05:28,428
Just right.

152
00:05:28,643 --> 00:05:31,468
Links that would last a million
years or more on their own?

153
00:05:31,593 --> 00:05:33,093
Broken while you chew.

154
00:05:33,113 --> 00:05:34,598
That sweet taste is you,

155
00:05:34,618 --> 00:05:36,658
tasting enzymes at work.

156
00:05:36,678 --> 00:05:38,148
It all comes down to shape.

157
00:05:38,373 --> 00:05:40,313
And that's how enzymes actually work.
